+<!-- ============ §1 Thesis ============ -->
+<h2 id="thesis">01 · The thesis</h2>
+<p class="lede">
+Most AI systems treat their layers as separate worlds: pixels live in shaders, embeddings live in HNSW, notes live in SQLite, claims live in a ledger, proofs live in Lean. Epistemos's thesis is that all of these are <em class="k">projections of one substrate</em>. The substrate is addressable. The projections are typed. The relationship between them is a system of theorems, not a stack of glue code.
+</p>
+<p>
+The mistake to avoid: collapsing the thesis into <em class="k">"everything is an EML tree."</em> EML (Elementary Morph Language) is one primitive among many. The substrate fabric is wider — it includes Geometry-IR, Scan-IR, Info-IR, Operator-IR, Tropical-IR, raw vectors, raw pixels, graph topology, and residency tiers.
+</p>
+
+<div class="note">
+  <strong>Canonical statement.</strong> Everything is one substrate object, expressed through different coordinates, planes, residencies, and primitive representations. Pixels, vectors, notes, graph nodes, KV pages, model components, AnswerPackets, tool results, and proofs are typed projections of the same addressable fabric. <em>— UNIFIED_ACTIVE_SUBSTRATE_CANON_2026_05_16</em>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============ §2 Layman ============ -->
+<h2 id="layman">02 · Layman explanation</h2>
+<div class="split">
+  <div class="split-tag">PLAIN</div>
+  <div class="split-body">A normal AI app is a pile of features that <em>look</em> related but don't actually know about each other. The chat doesn't know what the search did; the search doesn't know what the agent decided; the agent doesn't know what the editor edited. Epistemos forces them all to share one filing system — every note, every model output, every tool result, every search hit gets the same kind of ID — so the app can reason about itself, undo itself, and explain itself.</div>
+</div>
+<div class="split">
+  <div class="split-tag">PLAIN</div>
+  <div class="split-body">It also keeps an honest score. Every time the system approximates something (compresses a vector, fuses two searches, picks a model), the cost gets written down. Every time it shows you an answer, it shows you which notes that answer came from. If it makes up a citation, the system rejects the answer itself, not you.</div>
+</div>
+<div class="split">
+  <div class="split-tag">PLAIN</div>
+  <div class="split-body">And it runs locally. The cloud is an optional escalation, never a silent fallback. Your notes don't leave unless you ask them to.</div>
+</div>
+
